    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer, so all devices participate in *every* layer's computation.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts* (whole FFN blocks), while attention and other dense layers are typically replicated (or sharded via TP/DP). Devices participate only in the layers where their experts are routed.\n\nIn practice, production systems combine these: dense layers use TP (or DP with replicated weights), MoE layers use EP, and data parallelism (DP) sits on top for request-level scaling. This note covers the mechanics of both, their data flow, the collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention: $Y = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V \\cdot W_O$\n- MLP: $H = \\sigma(X W_1) W_2$ (or gated variant $H = (\\text{silu}(X W_g) \\odot X W_{up}) W_{down}$)\n\nThe canonical Megatron-style sharding:\n\n| Weight | Shard dimension | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | head dimension (column-parallel) | attention heads are independent; each rank computes a subset of heads |\n| $W_O$ | input dimension (row-parallel) | partial sums of head",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
